<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tacna</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tacna is a city in the far south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, near the Chilean border. Occupied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> in 1880, it remained under Chilean administration for nearly fifty years because the plebiscite promised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ancon">Treaty of Ancón</a> was never held. The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> of 1929 returned Tacna to Peru, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a> remained Chilean.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tacna.jpg" alt="Tacna" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marco Antonio Correa Flores · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Catedral_Nuestra_Se%C3%B1ora_del_Rosario_de_Tacna.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tacna, officially San Pedro de Tacna, lies in the Caplina River valley in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, about 35 km north of the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a>, and is the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of Tacna. The surrounding area is generally desert, with a few fertile spots near the mountains. The name is derived from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quechua">Quechua</a> words taka, meaning to hit, and na, meaning place. At the time of the Spanish conquest the region was multiethnic, with local sedentary populations making up about 66% of the people, settlers from the Altiplano about 25% and fishing people known as Camanchacos about 9%. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> system was established there in 1540.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1811 the royal accountant Francisco Antonio de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zela">Zela</a> started the push for Peruvian independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> at Tacna, and in 1828 President José de La Mar declared it the Heroic City. It was the capital of the short-lived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>–Bolivian Confederation from 1836 to 1839. Sodium nitrate and other resources brought prosperity and a wave of Italian immigrants. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a> ended this: a large Peruvian and Bolivian army was quartered in the city in poor sanitary conditions, which cost it much of its population to infectious diseases, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> captured it in May 1880 after defeating the allied army outside the city under General Manuel Baquedano.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-ancon">Treaty of Ancón</a> of 1883, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chile">Chile</a> was to administer the provinces of Tacna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arica">Arica</a> for ten years, after which a plebiscite would decide their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. When the ten years ended, the two sides could not agree whether to include a large number of imported Chilean labourers in the vote. Chilean authorities pursued a campaign of Chilenization, which Peruvian nationalists defeated, and the plebiscite was never held. Peruvian historiography calls the period the cautiverio. Tacna's patriotic Paseo de la Bandera began in 1901 when a group of its people defied a Chilean ban on showing Peruvian flags.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> of 1929 gave Chile Arica, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> recovered Tacna and received an indemnity of 6 million dollars and other concessions; the treaty took effect on 28 August 1929. Each year on 28 August a large Peruvian flag is carried through the city in the Paseo de la Bandera, which celebrates the return of Tacna to Peru. Tacna is a commercial city, with trade based on links with northern Chile, and it has monuments commemorating Peruvian independence and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-pacific">War of the Pacific</a>, among them the arch of the Alto de la Alianza complex and the Tacna Parabolic Arch. The Toquepala caves nearby have yielded some of the oldest human remains in Peru.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tacna” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…of the Pacific reshaped the map of South America, leaving Bolivia without a coastline, a loss Bolivia has never accepted, and giving Chile the provinces of Tarapacá and Antofagasta, while the dispute over <b>Tacna</b> and Arica was settled with Peru only by the Treaty of Lima of 1929.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Chile took Tarapacá, <b>Tacna</b> and Arica and occupied Lima in January 1881, while Andrés Avelino Cáceres led guerrilla resistance in the central highlands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru · Military</a></li></ul></section>
